---
import Breadcrumb from "../components/Breadcrumb.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../config/site";
import { webPageJsonLd } from "../lib/seo";

const title = "About Yardwork Calculators";
const description =
  "Yardwork Calculators estimates gravel, mulch, topsoil, and sod for US yards. Formulas and densities are published, and every density can be edited.";
const pathname = "/about/";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={webPageJsonLd(title, description, pathname)}>
  <header class="page-intro">
    <Breadcrumb current="About" />
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">{site.tagline}</p>
  </header>
  <article class="prose">
    <p>
      Yardwork is a set of calculators for people ordering landscape material in the United States:
      homeowners measuring a bed, and anyone else who has a length, a width, and a depth and needs
      cubic yards before calling a supplier. The first version covers gravel, mulch, topsoil, and
      sod.
    </p>
    <p>
      US customary units are the default because that is how the material is sold: cubic yards,
      short tons of 2,000 pounds, half-cubic-foot bags of stone, 2 and 3 cubic foot bags of mulch,
      and sod by the square foot and the pallet. A metric switch converts the measurements you
      type. The order units stay in the result.
    </p>
    <h2>What you can check</h2>
    <p>
      Each result includes the working, in the same order as the arithmetic: area, depth, net
      volume, allowance, density, weight, then bags or rolls. The
      <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> lists the formulas and the density behind every
      preset, with the reference it came from. Where a number is a midpoint of a published range
      rather than a single table value, the page says so.
    </p>
    <p>
      Densities are editable because moisture and the quarry move the weight. A preset gets the
      estimate started. The figure on a supplier’s ticket replaces it. Changing the material menu
      fills the preset back in, which the form tells you before you do it.
    </p>
    <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
    <p>
      It does not specify a driveway section, a wall, a drainage system, or a planting plan. Depth
      notes on the calculator pages are ordering guidance, and the driveway note is there to stop
      a 2 inch decorative calculation from being used as a base. It does not account for delivery
      minimums, tax, or the payload of a particular truck beyond showing the weight and telling you
      to read the sticker on the door.
    </p>
    <p>
      The calculators run in the browser on a static page. The measurements are not uploaded.
      There is no account. Contact details are on the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>, and
      they are still placeholders until this site is published under its own name and address.
    </p>
    <p>
      Material presets are reference figures, not paid placements. No supplier is featured, and
      the calculators do not send you to a store.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
